   338	
   339	> **Theorem R2.** Under the hypotheses of Lemma 3.15, ṽ ∉ δ(ãg̃₂).
   340	
   341	*Proof.*  Suppose ṽ ∈ δ(ãg̃₂).  Let q̃ be the interior vertex of ⟨ãg̃₂⟩ off
   342	which ṽ hangs and `B := br_q̃(ṽ)`, a connected component of δ(ãg̃₂).  Since
   343	g̃₁ ∉ Δ while g̃₂ ∈ Δ and Δ is a branch at ã, the path ⟨g̃₁g̃₂⟩ contains ã and
   344	then all of ⟨ãg̃₂⟩; in particular **q̃ ∈ ⟨g̃₁g̃₂⟩**, so Lemma 2.12 is available
   345	at ã := q̃, with Q₃ ∋ B.  By (S1) and [4], Lemma 4 every component of δ(ãg̃₂)
   346	other than B has positive determinant, so `det B > 0` too, and as in §3.3(3)
   347	`det Q₃ ⩾ 1`.  `det L̃∞ ⩽ 0` holds by Lemma DET-LINF.  By (S2) and (S4), B is
   348	either linear or a U⁰-component, and in the latter case q̃ = h̃ is the unique
   349	fork of Δ.
   350	
   351	**Case A: q̃ is not a fork** (so B is linear by (S2)).  Then `det(q̃g̃₂) = 2` by
   352	(S3).  Split on `det δ(q̃g̃₂)`, a positive integer (it avoids ṽ, [4], Lemma 4).
   353	
   354	*A1: `det δ(q̃g̃₂) ⩾ 2`.*  Then
   355	`(det δ(q̃g̃₂))²·det Q₃ ⩾ 4 > 2 = det(q̃g̃₂)`, so (2.13) holds with i = 2 and
   356	Lemma 2.12 gives ṽ ∈ Q₁ ∪ Q₂, contradicting ṽ ∈ B ⊆ Q₃.
   357	
   358	*A2: `det δ(q̃g̃₂) = 1`.*  Now Lemma 2.5's hypotheses hold verbatim at q̃
   359	(`det(q̃g̃₂) = 2`, `det δ(q̃g̃₂) = 1`, and q̃ ∈ ⟨g̃₂ṽ⟩ because ṽ hangs off q̃),
   360	so **q̃ is inessential for g̃₂**.  Since B is linear and `(ṽq̃) ⊆ B`, the
   361	subgraph (ṽq̃) is linear, so ⟨q̃ṽ⟩ has no nodal vertex other than q̃ and ṽ; ṽ
   362	is never essential (Definition 2.2 attaches no δ-component to a path endpoint)
   363	and q̃ is inessential for g̃₂ by the previous sentence.  Hence the extra
   364	hypothesis of Lemma 2.6 holds, **in its corrected g₂-form**
   365	(`REPAIRED[LEMMA-2.6-g1-TO-g2]`, consumed from the charged replay's R-4).
   366	Lemma 2.6 gives `det br_q̃(ṽ) < 0`, i.e. `det B < 0`, contradicting
   367	`det B > 0`.
