   370	`det B = 1`.  Compute `det(h̃g̃₂)`.  Since h̃ is the only fork of Δ, the chain
   371	from h̃ to g̃₂ has constant degree, equal to 2 by Lemma 1.5; hence by
   372	Definition 3.2 b), `det(h̃g̃₂) = (n(h̃)·n(g̃₂)/2)·det R_h = n(h̃)`, using
   373	`det R_h = 1`.  Also `Deg(h̃g̃₂) = m_p(h̃)·n(h̃) = 2` by [4], (1.16).
   374	
   375	*B1: n(h̃) = 1.*  Then `det(h̃g̃₂) = 1 ⩽ (det δ(h̃g̃₂))²·det Q₃`, since both
   376	factors are ⩾ 1 (the first avoids ṽ; the second by the paragraph above).
   377	Lemma 2.12 applies and contradicts ṽ ∈ Q₃ exactly as in Case A1.
   378	
   379	*B2: n(h̃) = 2.*  This case is **empty**.  Indeed then `m_p(h̃) = 1` on the
   380	g̃₂-side, and the chain from ã to h̃ is fork-free of constant degree 2, so the
   381	left edge at h̃ also has degree `m_{p′}(h̃)·n(h̃) = 2`, i.e. `m_{p′}(h̃) = 1`.
   382	By [4], (4) the multiplicities over each of the two horizontal directions sum
   383	to m(h̃), so each of those two directions carries at least two preimage points.
   384	Riemann–Hurwitz [4], (5) at h̃ reads
   385	`2(m(h̃) − 1) = Σ_{all p_j}(m_{p_j}(h̃) − 1) = ν(h)·m(h̃) − Σ_i k_i`, where the
   386	sum runs over the ν(h) directions at h in Γ_{L,g} and k_i is the number of
   387	preimage points in direction i.  With n(h̃) = 2, Deg(h̃) = 2m(h̃) ⩾ 4, so (3.1)
   388	forces `Deg(h̃) = 4`, `m(h̃) = 2`, and `h⁰ = {h̃}`.  If ν(h) = 3 the identity
   389	gives `Σ k_i = 4`, impossible since the two horizontal directions already
   390	contribute k ⩾ 2 each and the up-direction contributes k ⩾ 1.  If ν(h) = 4
