    53	## 1. Shared replay interface and D–O I bindings
    54	
    55	### 1.1 Determinant interface
    56	
    57	For vertices \(x,y\) in a weighted tree \(\Gamma\), the technical identity
    58	called “[4], (2)” is the edge-determinant identity
    59	
    60	\[
    61	 \det(xy)\det\Gamma
    62	 =\det\operatorname{br}_{x}(y)\det\operatorname{br}_{y}(x)
    63	  -\det(\Gamma-[xy])\det(\delta(xy))^2.                 \tag{E}
    64	\]
    65	
    66	Every use below specifies the ambient tree or branch.  The other technical
    67	imports bind as follows.
    68	
    69	| II citation | D–O I content used here | replay rule |
    70	|---|---|---|
    71	| [4], Lemma 2 | pairwise coprimality of branch determinants in a unimodular tree | used only after the branches and common vertex are identified |
    72	| [4], Lemma 3 | at the root all branch determinants are one; away from it all off-root branches but at most one are one | the root-facing branch is fixed before charging a unit determinant |
    73	| [4], Lemma 4 | a subgraph excluding the root has positive determinant | supplies positivity, not an exact value |
    74	| [4], Lemma 5; [4], (6), (8) | determinant transfer through a constant-degree component | the source component, its image, endpoint indices, and exceptional factor are displayed |
    75	| [4], (3) | canonical coefficient formula | invoked only at \(\widetilde g_1\) or \(\widetilde g_2\) after the vertex class and all branch data are fixed |
    76	| [4], (4), (5) | local degree sum and Riemann–Hurwitz | already charged in the repaired Fig. 8 census |
    77	
    78	The formulas used for the final canonical contradictions are REPLAY-1's
    79	checked Lemmas 2.9–2.11.  To prevent the source's index reuse from doing
    80	mathematical work, this report writes \(D_i\) for an essential-vertex degree
    81	in (2.5), (2.6), (2.11), or (2.12), and reserves \(d_i\) for a figure label.
    82	In particular, the \(d_2=2\) obtained in (7.11) is not the later canonical
    83	\(D_2=4\).
