codex
The direct-use scan is narrowing: the later proofs usually ask for a path to \( \widetilde g_2\) to be linear or for \(\det\delta(\cdot,\widetilde g_2)=1\) at an endpoint. I’m now separating those from any claim that \(\widetilde h\) itself is inessential.
codex
The visual check of Fig. 14 confirms that the \(b_2\)-uses of Lemma 3.15 put the possible fork at an endpoint of the \((b_2,g_2)\) delta, not inside the delta product. I’m checking the earlier Fig. 13 cases with the same rule.
codex
Fig. 13 likewise only forces \(\widetilde h\) into the displayed upper-port cases as an endpoint when \((\widetilde h,\widetilde g_2)\) is consumed. I’m checking the proofs around 5.6 and 6.5 because those are the places where a fork endpoint can accidentally become an interior delta factor.
codex
One important distinction is emerging: some later proofs do use \(\widetilde h\) being inessential, but they derive it directly from Lemma 2.5 in the \(\det(\widetilde h,\widetilde g_2)=2\) branch. That is separate from the open fork case in R2, where the determinant is \(1\).
codex
A separate issue is that R2′’s induction, as written, can only move along the \(\widetilde g_2\)-side until it reaches the fork. Past that point it would need the fork’s own inessentiality unless another argument is supplied. I’m checking whether any §§5-7 use asks for that past-fork segment.
